  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nYesterday was Halloween\u2014a structural headwind that typically depresses Sunday revenue by ~10%\u2014but Port Washington's real performance gap is deeper. After DOW adjustment, the store ran 10% below expectation at just one active groomer (Alyson D), delivering $420/groomer against a trailing-30-day baseline of $493. The concerning signal: add-on attach dropped from 78% to 71%, and retail fell 13%, suggesting softer upsell discipline and front-of-house focus. Hicksville, by contrast, overperformed its DOW-adjusted expectation significantly (+409%), driven by a retail surge (+202%) with one groomer (Anastasia C) staying at 92% of baseline productivity. The pattern: both locations are severely under-staffed in November relative to October (Port at 1 groomer-day vs. 6; Hicksville at 1 vs. 4), yet Hicksville's team compensated with stronger retail execution.\n\nWeek-to-date data shows zero Monday activity at both locations, which is expected post-Halloween when bookings lag. Month-to-date, Port Washington is tracking 71% below prior October due to staffing collapse, while Hicksville is running 51% below\u2014still a miss, but manageable. Your year-to-date performance remains strong: up 36% revenue and 34% appointments vs. 2025. Glen Cove remains retail-only and immaterial.\n\nOperationally, you have a $510k lapse pool (559 customers at Port, 220 at Hicksville) that represents genuine reactivation upside. Inventory at both locations is stretched thin\u2014X-Small Treat Tables and Cannoli are deeply negative\u2014which will limit retail upside if bookings improve.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today you've scheduled 5 groomers at Port and 1 at Hicksville (6 total); tomorrow 6 at Port and 3 at Hicksville (9 total); next 7 days you have 58 groomer-days scheduled across both locations, which restores capacity toward historical norms."
